      "value": "2026 bump\n\nThis is still one of the biggest gaps in Udon. Persistence helped with save data, but it can’t be set up before a player visits, players can wipe it, and it doesn’t carry across a creator’s worlds. Display names remain the only way to recognize someone, and they change constantly.\n\nWhat user IDs would enable\n\nEvent and staff whitelists: DJs, dancers, performers, and staff are whitelisted for stages and controls, and many change names often. IDs never break on a rename, can’t be faked by copying a name, and are easier to add, since you can just copy the ID from someone’s profile.\n\nPlayer-specific redeem codes and rewards that can’t be shared or reused by someone else.\n\nCreator Economy perks that line up with records kept outside VRChat, like Patreon tiers or event tickets.\n\nSave data that works like other games: Steam exposes SteamIDs and Roblox exposes UserIds. Persistence already ties data to the account internally, so we’re only asking for access to that identity.\n\nRepeatable gimmicks, like a love tester that gives the same result for the same two people, even after a name change.\n\nAddressing abuse concerns\n\nThe main worry is shared ban lists. Those are already against the rules, and anyone can compile a list of display names today. Withholding IDs doesn’t stop that. It only makes legitimate tools break.\n\nUser IDs are also already public in every VRChat.com profile URL. VRChat already shares VRC+ status and Creator Economy owner names with Udon, so this follows the same pattern.\n\nProposal\n\nExpose raw user IDs to Udon. This is the simplest and most useful option.\n\nIf more protection is needed, use a per-world ID: hash(userId + worldId). It stays consistent within a world but can’t be matched across worlds.\n\nEither way, let creators link their own worlds so the same player is recognized across a creator’s hub, event, and game worlds.\n\nOptionally limit access to verified creators, such as Creator Economy participants.\n\nWorld creators are already responsible for what we upload and the rules we follow. User IDs would let us run safer events, offer unique redeems and perks, and keep whitelists tied to the person instead of a name that can change at any time.",
